erberg.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/10/czi-rao-impact-report-25.pdf)\nciteturn90search12 [wordlim: 200] Published: last year; At the CZI Science in Society Meeting in 2023, networking sessions inspired Chelsea’s Hope’s clinical and research advisors to apply for an investigator-led safety study with the FDA to treat Lafora children with ION283, an antisense oligonucleotide therapy designed to halt disease progression. The study received FDA approval in August 2024, Chelsea’s Hope started fundraising to cover the clinical costs, and the first Lafora patient was enrolled in the trial in December of 2024.\nCaption below the photo: “110 attendees join the October 2023 Lafora Disease Science Symposium, co-hosted by Chelsea’s Hope, in person in Bologna.”\n</visual_element>\n\nDuring the Grant Period\n\nImpact Spotlight  \nChelsea’s Hope Lafora Children Research Fund was founded to provide support to Lafora families and accelerate the path to treatment. Families built relationships with researchers and clinicians to drive therapy development. However, the organization struggled to transition therapies from pre-clinical experiments to clinical trials. In the two years prior to the grant, three separate pharma companies had decided to halt their Lafora therapy programs.\n\nIn Lafora disease, early intervention is critical to halt progression before irreversible neurodegeneration occurs. The RAO grant allowed Chelsea’s Hope to hire staff dedicated to expanding their network. Over three years, the organization more than tripled the number of families connected to the organization and built an international coalition with sister organizations in Europe. Through this coalition, Chelsea’s Hope was empowered to expand participation in an international registry, engage more than 20 new clinicians, and establish an Advisory Board to create recommendations for clinical trial design.\n\nAt the CZI Science in Society Meeting in 2023, networking sessions inspired Chelsea’s Hope’s clinical and research advisors to apply for an investigator-led safety study with the FDA to treat Lafora children with ION283, an antisense oligonucleotide therapy designed to halt disease progression. The study received FDA approval in August 2024, Chelsea’s Hope started fundraising to cover the clinical costs, and the first Lafora patient was enrolled in the trial in December of 2024. This accelerated pathway to the clinic was made possible through the capacity development Chelsea’s Hope received through the RAO grant.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSafety Study Press Release February 2025 (https://chelseashope.org/wp-content/uploads/2025/02/Safety-Study-Press-Release-February-2025.pdf)\nciteturn90search13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4 months ago; FIRST PATIENTS RECEIVE ION283 DOSING FOR LAFORA DISEASE ... In December 2024, the first patient received their first dose of ION283, a treatment designed to\nPress Release February 20, 2025 \nFOR IMMEDIATE RELEASE \nFIRST PATIENTS RECEIVE ION283 DOSING FOR LAFORA DISEASE \nThe groundbreaking safety study is happening at UTSW Medical Center in Dallas. \nSACRAMENTO, Calif. – February 20, 2025 – The first-ever Lafora disease safety study at the \nUniversity of Texas Southwestern Medical Center (USTW) will continue enrolling patients in \nMarch.  \nLafora is a neurodegenerative disease affecting an estimated 250 children worldwide. It is a \ngenetic condition in which patients cannot maintain a normal glycogen concentration, resulting \nin a toxic accumulation of glycogen, or Lafora Bodies, in the heart, spine, and brain. Symptoms \ninclude epilepsy, ataxia, childhood dementia, and difficulty speaking, walking, and eating. There \nis no cure, although the safety study is providing hope for the worldwide community. \nIn December 2024, the first patient received their first dose of ION283, a treatment designed to \nhalt disease progression. In January 2025, the first patient comp